Centinela is the eleventh planet from the star Aurelius, orbiting in the cold, dark reaches of the outer system between the white giant [[Lucero]] and the rocky outlier [[Erebo]]. It is the second of the two ice giants in the Aurelius system. Despite being slightly smaller in radius than Lucero, it is more massive, making it the third-most massive planet in the entire system.

Centinela is characterized by its deep cobalt blue color, a result of the specific concentration of methane and trace hydrocarbons in its upper atmosphere that absorb red light more efficiently than the atmosphere of Lucero. It is surrounded by a system of incredibly sharp, bright icy rings that are significantly more reflective than the dark carbon rings of [[Amatista]].

As the last major planet before the system's edge, Centinela serves as a gravitational guardian, stabilizing the orbits of outer debris and the planet Erebo. Its name reflects its perceived role as a silent sentinel watching over the void.

==Discovery==
The existence of a planet beyond [[Lucero]] was mathematically predicted by several Adonian astronomers who noted unexplained perturbations in Lucero's orbit. On September 23, 1846 (Adonian Era), Sanese astronomer Kaito Hoshino (星野 凱斗), using calculations provided by Insulonian mathematicians, identified the planet within one degree of its predicted position.

The discovery was made at the Mount Zenran Observatory during a period of exceptional atmospheric clarity. Hoshino described the planet as a tiny, blue-tinged sapphire drifting against the starfields of the southern celestial hemisphere. This successful prediction and subsequent discovery was hailed as a triumph of celestial mechanics and the Gakuen Principle of orbital harmony.

==Physical characteristics==
===Atmosphere===
The atmosphere of Centinela is composed primarily of hydrogen and helium, with a significant enrichment of methane and ammonia ices. The deep blue hue is attributed to high-altitude clouds of methane ice that are more densely packed than those found on Lucero.

Unlike the featureless Lucero, Centinela exhibits visible atmospheric activity, including faint dark wind streaks and high-altitude white clouds composed of methane cirrus. These clouds move at extreme speeds, driven by some of the fastest winds recorded in the system, occasionally exceeding 2,000 km/h.

===Ring system===
Centinela possesses the most visually striking ring system of the four giant planets. The rings are composed of nearly pure water ice, giving them a high albedo. They are notably narrow and sharp-edged. This precision is maintained by two tiny shepherd moons, Talos and Vigil, which orbit on the inner and outer edges of the primary ring structure, using their gravity to prevent the ring particles from drifting away.

==Orbit and rotation==
Centinela orbits Aurelius at an average distance of 35.40 AU, completing one revolution every 78,000 standard days (approximately 213 Adonian years). Because of its immense orbital period, only a fraction of its path has been observed since its formal discovery in 1846.

The planet rotates on its axis once every 16.1 hours. Its axial tilt is 28 degrees, giving it seasonal cycles similar in orientation to [[Adonia]] and [[Aztlán]], though the sheer distance from the star means that seasonal temperature variations are minimal in the absolute sense.

==Name and etymology==
The name Centinela is Insulonian for sentinel or guard, referring to its position at the edge of the major planetary system. In Sanese astronomical nomenclature, it is known as Morobitosei (守人星 [Morobitosei]), which translates to the Guardian Star or Protector Star. Both names signify the planet's cultural role as the final outpost of the Aurelius system's main family of planets.

